"""
Code-division receiver: multiplies the wire by its own spreading code and averages the product
over each bit (integrate-and-dump).

`correlation` is the running integral of `wire * code` divided by `bit_time`. It is dumped to
zero during a short window at the start of each bit, so it ramps through the bit and ends at the
bit's average. `bit` samples that value in a window at the end of each bit and holds it through
the next bit, so it shows each received bit one bit time late. Codes orthogonal to this
receiver's code integrate to zero over a bit and drop out.
"""
component Correlator
  "Shared wire"
  wire = RealInput() {^wire}
  "This receiver's spreading code, +1 or -1 per chip"
  code = RealInput() {^code}
  "Running average of wire times code over the current bit"
  correlation = RealOutput() {^correlation}
  "Last completed bit's average, held for one bit"
  bit = RealOutput() {^bit}
  "Duration of one bit"
  parameter bit_time::Time = 1.0
  "Dump and sample windows as a fraction of the bit"
  parameter window::Real = 0.02
  "Dump and sample time constant, much shorter than a window"
  parameter tau::Time = window * bit_time / 10
  "Integral of wire times code since the last dump"
  variable integral::Real
  "Position within the current bit, 0 to 1"
  variable phase::Real
relations
  initial integral = 0.0
  initial bit = 0.0
  phase = time / bit_time - floor(time / bit_time)
  der(integral) = wire * code - ifelse(phase < window, integral / tau, 0.0)
  correlation = integral / bit_time
  der(bit) = ifelse(phase > 1 - window, (correlation - bit) / tau, 0.0)
end
